This year's Edmonton summer has been quite something.

From June 1 to today (July 27), Environment Canada recorded **511 mm of rain** in Edmonton — the highest volume since 1953. For context, Edmonton typically sees a monthly average of 70–80 mm and an annual average of 423 mm. In under two months, we've already blown past a full year's worth of rain.

The effects are hard to miss: mosquitoes are rampant, and road and basement flooding have become common. The water table is high, the ground is saturated, and sump pumps across the city are running around the clock — including mine.

## How a Sump Pump Actually Works

All this pump activity got me looking into how the system works. Water reaches the sump pit in two ways:

- **Perforated foundation pipe:** A perforated pipe runs around the house's foundation and channels nearby water to an exit pipe roughly two feet from the bottom of the pit, which then drains into it.
- **Ground saturation:** When the water table rises above the bottom of the pit, two holes at the base slowly let water seep in — a safeguard against basement flooding. Once the water level reaches the pump's float switch, the pump kicks in and sends the water out to the municipal storm ponds through the storm drainage system on the side of the house.

## Why My Pump Runs So Often

My particular setup has two quirks that make it work harder than it needs to:

1. **No check valve** — a check valve normally seals shut after pumping to stop water from flowing back down into the pit. Mine doesn't have one, so some water flows back in after each cycle.
2. **Vertical internal float** — rather than a tethered float, mine uses an internal vertical float. It triggers with less water, which means more frequent cycles — and it can't easily be tested without pouring water in to raise the level manually.

Together, these mean my pump runs noticeably more often than my neighbors' units. During and after the heaviest rain days (30+ mm at a time), it was cycling roughly once a minute. Water from the perforated pipe system keeps showing up for at least three days after a rainfall, and groundwater seepage continues until the water table drops below the bottom of the pit.

## Time for a Replacement

Ten years in, my sump pump has been a quiet hero, working tirelessly to keep the basement dry. I'm planning to replace it — either by the end of this season or at the start of the next.

I've gathered a couple of quotes so far:

- One plumbing company quoted **$800** for a Liberty 1/3 HP pump, check valve, and installation. Liberty is regarded as one of the best sump pump brands out there.
- An independent plumber quoted **$425** for a Burcam pump, which is decent quality.

Pumps generally run **$200–$400**, with the rest being labor. I could DIY the install, but I'm wary of getting it wrong on something this important.

For now, fingers crossed that our sump pump keeps doing its job for the rest of the year.